# Every variable here is OPTIONAL.
#
# With no .env file at all, Waypoint runs against the bundled fixtures in
# src/data/fixtures — the full UI works offline, including adding, editing and
# reviewing a place, which is kept in localStorage. Sign-in is hidden, because
# there is nothing to sign in to. That is the default `npm run dev` experience.
#
# Fill these four in to read and write live data in Firestore and to enable
# Google sign-in. Copy them from the Firebase console:
#   Project settings -> General -> Your apps -> SDK setup and configuration
#
# The web API key is a public client identifier, not a secret: it ships in the
# JavaScript bundle either way, and what a caller may do is decided by
# firestore.rules. It lives here only so the repository is not tied to one
# Firebase project.
#
# There is no storage bucket in this list on purpose. Photographs are links to
# images already on the web, so nothing in the app talks to Cloud Storage — and
# since February 2026 a bucket needs the paid Blaze plan, which this project
# does not use.

VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# Which shape to read. Leave unset for the schema this project seeds with
# scripts/seed-firestore.mjs: one document per place, reviews beneath it.
# Set to `legacy` to read the inherited 2021 database instead — places split
# across two collections, reviews joined by storage folder name, read-only.
# VITE_FIREBASE_SCHEMA=legacy

# Basemap tiles. Defaults to CARTO Positron for light and Esri Dark Gray Canvas
# for dark, neither of which needs a key. The map's light/dark control switches
# between the two, so override both or the switch will only work one way.
# Each URL carries its own credit; override the attribution with it.
# VITE_TILE_URL=
# VITE_TILE_ATTRIBUTION=
# VITE_TILE_URL_DARK=
# VITE_TILE_ATTRIBUTION_DARK=
